  "question": {
    "text": "Example of a three-dimensional silicate is :<br />\r&nbsp;"
  },
  "options": [
    {
      "text": "Ultramarines",
      "is_correct": false
    },
    {
      "text": "Feldspars",
      "is_correct": false
    },
    {
      "text": "Zeolites",
      "is_correct": false
    },
    {
      "text": "All of these&nbsp;",
      "is_correct": true
    }
  ],
  "correct_answer": [
    "D"
  ],
  "solution": {
    "text": "<p>Three-dimensional silicates are formed by sharing all four oxygen atom of&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msup><msub><mi>SiO</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo>-</mo></mrow></msup></math>. Ultramarines, feldspars and zeolites are examples of three- dimensional silicates. Molecular formula of ultramarine is&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>Na</mi><mn>8</mn></msub><msub><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mi>SiAlO</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow><mn>6</mn></msub></math>&nbsp;feldspar is&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>KAlSi</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mn>8</mn></msub></math>&nbsp;and zeolite is&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>Na</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><msub><mi>Al</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><msub><mi>Si</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mn>8</mn></msub><mo>.</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><msub><mi>xH</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi></math>.</p>"
  }
